---
.title = "Write your first ZML model",
.description = "Define a tagged linear layer, compile it for CPU, and read its output back on the host.",
.date = .date("2026-11-16T00:00:00"),
.layout = "content.shtml",
---
Build a linear layer with three input features and two output scores. The
example uses fixed weights so you can calculate the expected scores by hand.
Use the checkout and Bazel version from the
[ZML quickstart]($link.page('zml/quickstart')).

# [1. Describe the computation]($heading.id('describe-the-computation'))

The model is a Zig struct with `zml.Tensor` fields and a `forward` method.
Each tensor describes a shape and data type. Assigning a tag to an axis lets
the operation select that axis by name.

| Tensor | Type and shape | Axis tags |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `input` | `f32[3]` | `feature` |
| `weight` | `f32[2, 3]` | `output`, `feature` |
| `bias` | `f32[2]` | `output` |
| Result | `f32[2]` | `output` |

`weight.dot(input, .feature)` sums along the three features for each weight
row. The result has two output scores. Adding `bias` adds one value to each
score.

# [2. Compile and call the layer]($heading.id('compile-and-call-the-layer'))

In your ZML checkout, create `examples/first_model/main.zig` with this code.

```zig
const std = @import("std");
const zml = @import("zml");

const Linear = struct {
    weight: zml.Tensor,
    bias: zml.Tensor,

    pub fn forward(self: Linear, input: zml.Tensor) zml.Tensor {
        return self.weight.dot(input, .feature).add(self.bias);
    }
};

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !void {
    const allocator = init.gpa;
    const io = init.io;
    const platform: *zml.Platform = try .auto(allocator, io, .{});
    defer platform.deinit(allocator, io);

    const layer: Linear = .{
        .weight = zml.Tensor.init(.{ 2, 3 }, .f32).withTags(.{ .output, .feature }),
        .bias = zml.Tensor.init(.{2}, .f32).withTags(.{.output}),
    };
    const input = zml.Tensor.init(.{3}, .f32).withTags(.{.feature});
    var executable = try platform.compile(allocator, io, layer, .forward, .{input}, .{});
    defer executable.deinit();

    const weights = [2][3]f32{ .{ 1, 2, 3 }, .{ 4, 5, 6 } };
    const biases = [2]f32{ 1, -1 };
    const features = [3]f32{ 1, 2, 1 };
    const weight_values = zml.Slice.init(layer.weight.shape(), std.mem.asBytes(&weights));
    const bias_values = zml.Slice.init(layer.bias.shape(), std.mem.asBytes(&biases));
    const input_values = zml.Slice.init(input.shape(), std.mem.asBytes(&features));

    var weight_buffer = try zml.Buffer.fromSlice(io, platform, weight_values);
    defer weight_buffer.deinit();
    var bias_buffer = try zml.Buffer.fromSlice(io, platform, bias_values);
    defer bias_buffer.deinit();
    var input_buffer = try zml.Buffer.fromSlice(io, platform, input_values);
    defer input_buffer.deinit();
    const layer_buffers: zml.Bufferized(Linear) = .{
        .weight = weight_buffer,
        .bias = bias_buffer,
    };

    var args = try executable.args(allocator);
    defer args.deinit(allocator);
    args.set(.{ layer_buffers, input_buffer });

    var results = try executable.results(allocator);
    defer results.deinit(allocator);
    executable.call(io, args, &results, .{ .wait = true });

    var output_buffer = results.get(zml.Buffer);
    defer output_buffer.deinit();
    const scores = try output_buffer.getValue([2]f32, io);
    std.debug.print("Scores: {d:.1}, {d:.1}\n", .{ scores[0], scores[1] });
}
```

`platform.compile` traces `forward` using symbolic tensors and returns an
executable for the platform. After compilation, each `zml.Slice` borrows the
host bytes for a concrete tensor. `Buffer.fromSlice` copies those bytes into
a platform buffer. `zml.Bufferized(Linear)` keeps the model's structure and
places `zml.Buffer` handles in its tensor fields.

The caller owns the input and weight buffers. `args.set` uses those handles
and `results.get` retrieves the output buffer. Release each buffer once, as
shown by the `defer` statements. Keep the platform alive until the executable
and its buffers have been released. `getValue` copies the two scores back to
the host.

# [3. Run the model]($heading.id('run-the-model'))

Add `examples/first_model/BUILD.bazel` next to the Zig file.

```starlark
load("@rules_zig//zig:defs.bzl", "zig_binary")

zig_binary(
    name = "first_model",
    main = "main.zig",
    deps = ["//zml"],
)
```

From the checkout root, run the new target. The quickstart configuration
enables CPU by default.

```sh
bazel run //examples/first_model
```

The expected output line is `Scores: 9.0, 19.0`. Those values come from the
dot products `1 + 4 + 3` and `4 + 10 + 6`, with biases of `1` and `-1`.
Try a different `features` array and calculate the new scores before running
the target again.

# [Load weights from a checkpoint]($heading.id('load-weights-from-a-checkpoint'))

The [MNIST example](https://github.com/zml/zml/blob/8fcff8c462070d06056f8a85ba0c8a81f0f3a636/examples/mnist/mnist.zig)
uses the same sequence for a pretrained model. It opens a
[Safetensors registry]($link.page('zml/checkpoints').ref('load-weights-for-execution')),
uses `TensorStore` to create tagged weight tensors from the checkpoint shapes,
then compiles `forward`. A loader fills the model's buffers with the weight
data. It waits for those transfers before passing the buffers to the
executable. The output is a predicted digit read back as `u8`.

The example above follows the pinned ZML source APIs. Execution of the new
target remains pending.
